CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ enumerator through a later member typedef: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ the other enumerator: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
N5008 [basic.lookup.qual.general]/1, [dcl.enum]/11: a
nested-name-specifier may denote an enumeration, and a typedef-name
naming one does too, so `action::copy' names the enumerator.  The front
end followed a member typedef used as a qualifier only when it named a
class, so `action::' found no scope and the member functions using it
were not type-checked.  The declaration order (the typedef is declared
after the functions, in the complete-class context of their bodies,
[class.mem.general]/7) is not what failed: the same happens with the
typedef declared first, while a namespace-scope typedef worked.
libstdc++'s std::function manager has this shape.  Fails on the round-163
build bin52.
